{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>Now Rachel had stolen the terafim.</b> She intended only to separate her father from idolatrous worship — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 31:19). And it is possible that they belonged to Laban for idolatrous worship, as he said, \"Why have you stolen my elohim?\" But not all terafim were objects of worship, for how could idolatrous worship be found in the house of our lord David? (see I Samuel 19:13). And the closest explanation is what they say, that they are instruments for receiving the hours, and they would divine with them to know the future, and the word is derived from the language of \"רְפֵה יָדַיִם\" (weakening of hands) (Isaiah 2:17b?) — \"נִרְפִּים אַתֶּם נִרְפִּים\" (Exodus 5:17): they called them \"terafim\" to hint in their name that their speech is like weak prophecy, which comes in abundance and occasionally lies, as they said, \"For the terafim have spoken vanity\" (Zechariah 10:2). And so people of little faith would make them into elohim for themselves; they would not inquire in the honored Name nor pray to Him, but rather all their deeds would be by the divinations that the terafim would tell them. And it is written, \"And the man Micah had a house of elohim, and he made an ephod and terafim\" (Judges 17:5), and there it is written, \"Inquire now of elohim, that we may know whether our way will prosper\" (Judges 18:5), for it was by the terafim that they would inquire. And so the matter was in Israel with the ephod: after they grew accustomed to the holy ephod, they made something like its form and would inquire of it and trust its words and go astray after it, and even in their illnesses they would not seek YHWH but only them. And this is the meaning of \"And Gideon made it into an ephod and set it up in his city, in Ophrah, and all Israel went astray after it there, and it became a snare to Gideon and to his house\" (Judges 8:27), for they turned away from following YHWH. Now Laban was a diviner and soothsayer, as he said, \"I divined\" (Genesis 30:27), and his land was a land of diviners from of old, as it is written, \"For they are filled from the east and with soothsayers like the Philistines\" (Isaiah 2:6), and Balaam son of Beor the diviner was from his city. And this is the meaning of \"Why have you stolen my elohim?\":"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:37:44.938715028Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}